From 2cdef09b665c7df7645c44c015a8f2d0c3926320 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: autoresearch <agent@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 03:59:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] ggml: stop splitting tiny elementwise ops across every
 thread

CPY/DUP/CONT/ADD/ADD_ID/ADD1/ACC/CUMSUM/TRI/FILL were the only ops whose task count was unconditionally
n_threads, with no work-size test - unlike SOFT_MAX, which already does MIN(n_threads, nrows). On the 2-A78
mask that made a 17 KB elementwise add cost 56 us (~300 MB/s): two threads each move 8.5 KB and then wait
at a barrier. The same tensors through single-threaded SCALE moved 29 KB in 4.4 us (6.7 GB/s). The x-asr leg
issues ~107k ADD and ~83k CONT calls per 69 s clip, so the barrier tax was a large share of a leg that is
only 33% MUL_MAT.

These ops now go single-threaded below 32768 elements and keep both threads above it. Row partitioning
decides which core runs which row and changes nothing else - same elements, same order - and the host gate
confirms it: transcript hash, all three WERs, attribution and DER-lite unchanged.

This ggml belongs to CrispASR only (audiocpp keeps its own hidden copy), so the effect is attributable to
the ASR leg alone. Re-apply after any ggml bump.
---
 src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
index b11e3d1..7bcd349 100644
--- a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -2280,7 +2280,13 @@ static int ggml_get_n_tasks(struct ggml_tensor * node, int n_threads) {
         case GGML_OP_TRI:
         case GGML_OP_FILL:
             {
-                n_tasks = n_threads;
+                // Work-size threshold, in the spirit of the GGML_OP_SOFT_MAX case below (MIN(n_threads,
+                // nrows)). These ops were the only ones unconditionally split across every thread, so a
+                // 17 KB elementwise add paid a two-thread dispatch plus a barrier to move 8.5 KB - measured
+                // on a 2-A78 mask at 56 us/call and ~300 MB/s, while a 29 KB single-threaded SCALE on the
+                // same tensors ran at 4.4 us and 6.7 GB/s. Partitioning the rows changes which core does
+                // which row and nothing else: same elements, same order, bit-identical output.
+                n_tasks = ggml_nelements(node) >= 32768 ? n_threads : 1;
             } break;
         case GGML_OP_SUB:
         case GGML_OP_SQR:
